.TH SEGMENT 1
.SH NAME
segment \- runs of a text with even bits a rune
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B segment
[
.B -p
.I penalty
] [
.B -l
.I runes
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Segment
reads the lines
.IR bits (1)
prints, one a token, from its standard input, and splits the text into segments of nearly even
bits a rune, where the text's density changes.
It prints a line for each segment,
.IP
.I "q0 q1 bpc"
.LP
its rune offsets and its bits a rune, the sum of its tokens' bits over its runes, to two places.
The segments tile the text.
.PP
The method is PELT, by Killick, Fearnhead and Eckley, whose time is about linear in the text.
A segment's cost is, for each of its tokens, the square of the token's bits a rune less the
segment's, times the token's runes, and each segment costs the
.I penalty
besides (100 by default).
A larger penalty gives fewer, longer segments.
No segment is shorter than
.I runes
(16 by default), unless the whole text is.
.PP
The least length keeps one costly token, such as a misspelling's, out of a segment of its own:
a span of
.I L
runes that costs
.I B
bits more than the text around it gains about
.if t \fIB\fP\u\s-22\s+2\d/\fIL\fP
.if n B^2/L
by standing alone, against two penalties for its two cuts.
At 16 runes and the default penalty, a token would need some 57 bits more than its neighbors,
more than a model gives one token, and a token is rarely longer than 16 runes;
a phrase or a sentence is longer, and still makes a segment.
A spike is for the tool that reads the tokens, as
.IR huh (1)
does, not for
.IR segment .
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
% bits doc/words.md | segment | sort -nr +2 | sed 3q
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B src/cmd/segment.c
.br
.B src/cmd/huh/pelt.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bits (1),
.IR huh (1),
.IR lmsim (4)
.SH BUGS
The default penalty and least length are guesses, until a real model's scores set them.
